<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodemir (Visigoth)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 743</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodemir was a Visigothic noble who governed south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> at the time of the Muslim conquest. After resisting the invaders he agreed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orihuela">Orihuela</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-ibn-musa">Abd al-Aziz ibn Musa</a> in 713, keeping control of seven towns in return for tribute. His land became known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> as Tudmir, after his name, and survived as an autonomous Christian district for several decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodemir-visigoth.jpg" alt="Theodemir (Visigoth)" width="183" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roxanna · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teodomiro_de_Orihuela.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theodemir, also written Theudimer, was a comes, or count, prominent in the south-east of the province of Carthaginensis, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, during the last decades of the Visigothic kingdom. Probably during the joint reign of Egica and Wittiza he drove off a Byzantine fleet that had raided the coasts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. The date is disputed: it may have belonged to Leontios's expedition to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 697, or have come around 702 or late in Wittiza's reign. Most historians see it as an isolated incident, not an attempt to recover the province of Spania lost in the 620s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a> was defeated at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a>, Theodemir resisted the Arab invaders until he was beaten in a pitched battle and made peace. The treaty, kept in at least three sources and preserved by the Andalusian historian Ibn Adarí in the 13th century, named seven cities: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orihuela">Orihuela</a>, Valentila, perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, Mula, Bigastro, Eyya, probably Ojós, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorca">Lorca</a>. Christians who submitted kept their lives, families, customs and churches, but each paid a yearly tribute of one dinar with measures of wheat, barley, grape juice, vinegar, honey and oil, half as much for slaves, and they had to hand over the conquerors' enemies.</p><p>Theodemir kept his land and local authority, and he later travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to have the treaty confirmed by the Umayyad caliph. How long it held in practice, whether until his death or not, is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His death is recorded in the Chronicle of 754, which describes his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanagild">Athanagild</a> as very wealthy. Scholars debate whether Athanagild succeeded him; if he did, it was around 740, and his fate is unknown. Tudmir had lost its independence by the 780s. Later Gothic nobles of the region tried to claim descent from Theodemir, a sign of his standing, and the novel Amaya o los vascos en el siglo VIII of 1879 names him among those who resisted Islamic rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theodemir (Visigoth)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His superior Musa ibn Nusayr followed in 712, and within a few years most of the peninsula had been conquered, often by treaties with local lords such as <b>Theodemir</b> of Tudmir.</blockquote><a href="/state/al-andalus-umayyad-province">Al-Andalus (Umayyad province)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Theodemir</b>, a Visigothic noble who governed the south-east, resisted the Muslim invaders and then negotiated.</blockquote><a href="/state/tudmir">Tudmir</a></li></ul></section>
